    {"step": 1, "tactic": "UDP 5355 (LLMNR) bind by powershell.exe", "technique": "T1557.001", "what_happened": "At 2034-02-19 16:18:08 UTC on WS-MKT-31, AMSI EID 4104 captured a powershell.exe scriptblock 'New-Object System.Net.Sockets.UdpClient(5355)' AND Sysmon EID 3 confirmed UDP 5355 bind by powershell.exe. Rule 61603 triggered.", "chain_reason": "UDP 5355 is the LLMNR (Link-Local Multicast Name Resolution) port. Bound legitimately ONLY by svchost.exe (DNS Client service) on every Windows host. ANY OTHER process binding 5355 is the Responder/Inveigh poisoning primitive — the attacker becomes a fake LLMNR responder that answers ANY hostname query with their own IP, redirecting victim SMB/HTTP connections to the attacker."},
    {"step": 2, "tactic": "UDP 137 (NBT-NS) bind by powershell.exe", "technique": "T1557.001", "what_happened": "At 2034-02-19 16:18:24, AMSI EID 4104 captured 'New-Object System.Net.Sockets.UdpClient(137)' AND Sysmon EID 3 confirmed UDP 137 bind. Rule 61603 secondary fire.", "chain_reason": "UDP 137 is NetBIOS Name Service (NBT-NS). Same poisoning role as LLMNR but for older NetBIOS clients. Binding BOTH 5355 + 137 from a non-svchost process gives full LAN name-resolution-poisoning coverage."},
    {"step": 3, "tactic": "LLMNR multicast lure query to 224.0.0.252:5355 + TCP 445 listener bind", "technique": "T1557.001", "what_happened": "At 2034-02-19 16:18:40, AMSI EID 4104 captured an LLMNR query packet for 'fileserv' (typo'd hostname) sent via UDP to 224.0.0.252:5355 multicast group. AMSI also captured 'New-Object System.Net.Sockets.TcpListener([System.Net.IPAddress]::Any, 445)' AND $listener.Start() — the NetNTLM capture endpoint. Rule 61605 triggered.", "chain_reason": "Sending an LLMNR query for a misspelled hostname AND simultaneously listening on TCP 445 = full Responder workflow. The lure query gets multicast back to ALL hosts (including future legitimate victims). Whenever any LAN client types a typo'd hostname OR DNS fails, they will get the attacker IP from LLMNR and connect to TCP 445 — delivering NetNTLM hash."},
    {"step": 4, "tactic": "AMSI Invoke-Inveigh/Responder tool literals + LLMNR enable-state recon", "technique": "T1557.001", "what_happened": "At 2034-02-19 16:18:52, Sysmon EID 1 captured reg.exe query 'HKLM\\SOFTWARE\\Policies\\Microsoft\\Windows NT\\DNSClient' /v EnableMulticast — checking if LLMNR is enabled on the host. AMSI EID 4104 captured scriptblock literals: 'Invoke-Inveigh -ConsoleOutput Y -SMB Y -HTTP Y -LLMNR Y -NBNS Y', 'responder.py -I eth0 -wrf', 'ntlmrelayx.py -t smb://10.0.0.50 -smb2support', 'mitm6 -d corp.local --ignore-nofqdn'. Rule 92300 triggered.", "chain_reason": "4 named LLMNR/NBT-NS poisoning tool literals + EnableMulticast reg read = full T1557.001 toolkit awareness. EnableMulticast=0 means the corp GPO has DISABLED LLMNR fleet-wide (good), so the attacker is hoping at least some hosts haven't received the policy yet."}
